+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+`src/index.ts` already has `applyPatch`, `cloneJson`, `jsonEqual` (RFC 6902 §4.6) and safe member setting. No dependencies may be added.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:** correct round trip; small patches; safe with hostile keys; no shared structure.
+
+**Non-Goals:** `copy`/`test` ops, global-minimal move detection, handling non-JSON values.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Recursive diff.** Equal values give no ops; differing objects recurse per member; differing arrays use the array algorithm; anything else (type change, differing primitives) is one `replace` (path `""` at root, which RFC 6902 §4.3 permits).
+- **Arrays via LCS.** Trim common prefix/suffix, then LCS over `jsonEqual` on the middle. Walking the result left to right with a running index, runs between matches pair up items positionally (recursive diff in place) and any surplus becomes `remove` (index stays) or `add` (index advances). Indices are therefore valid for ordered application (§3). Moves become remove+add, which the request allows; RFC `move` is not used, as it is not needed for the bound and LCS cannot tell a move from delete+insert cheaply.
+- **Pairing in place** can nest a diff into an item that was really replaced; the output is still correct and no larger than replace.
+- **Order of object ops:** removes, then in-place changes, then adds; all independent paths.
+- **Pointer escaping** `~`→`~0` first, then `/`→`~1` (RFC 6901 §3). Use `Object.keys`/own-member checks and clone values with `cloneJson` so `__proto__` is safe and nothing is shared.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+LCS is O(n·m) in the differing middle of arrays; prefix/suffix trimming keeps the common case cheap. Very large, wholly different arrays are costly.
